----------------------
- What is Handy/SDL? -
----------------------

Handy is software dedicated to emulating the Atari Lynx hardware on a standard
PC. The software was originally developed by Keith Wilkins [http://handy.sf.net]
and was released under the GPL on 14th April 2004. 

Handy/SDL is a port of the WIN32 sourcecode so that it can be used with the
GCC compiler and the Simple Directmedia Layer (SDL) library.

Currently Handy/SDL compiles on WIN32 using mingw, and possible also on
UN*X based systems (BeOS, Linux and FreeBSD). It may run on other systems
as well but we can not guarantee that.

The display now uses the SDL2 render API. The window can be resized freely
and the picture is scaled to fit on the GPU with the aspect ratio preserved.
Use -scale N for the initial size and -smooth if you prefer filtered scaling
over sharp pixels.

The old rendering backends and their options are gone: the OpenGL and YUV
overlay paths (-rtype, -fsaa, -accel, -sync, -format), the software scalers
and filters (-stype, -filter, -lcd) and the bit depth selector (-bpp). SDL2
does the scaling in hardware, which is what most of them existed to provide.

The port was done by the SDLEMU crew (http://sdlemu.ngemu.com) and especially
by Niels Wagenaar and Carwin Jones. You may contact us by e-mail using
sdlemu@ngemu.com or leave a message on the SDLEMU Official Forum (see the website). 
Patches and bugfixes are particularly welcome! :)

More information about SDL can be found at http://www.libsdl.org.

Note that the OpenSource release is not quite as 'advanced' as the originally
released Handy/SDL v0.82-R1. The sources have been lost and everything was
done from scratch. Please be patient for newer releases with more functionality.

-------------------
- Using Handy/SDL -
-------------------

After compiling the binary (see INSTALL) you can now run original Atari Lynx
games. These games are distributed on cartridges and can therefore cannot be
used on a PC. To use these games on Handy/SDL you need to get ROM images.
Besides the ROM images from (commercial) games you must also obtain the
Atari Lynx boot ROM which you must copy to the root directory where the binary
resides. For legal issues concerning ROM images please consult the DISCLAIMER
at the beginning of this file.

After obtaining the Atari Lynx boot ROM and several ROMs you can now use 
Handy/SDL using the following command:

./handy_sdl [romfile] [options]

The romfile is optional. Started with no arguments, Handy/SDL comes up with
no cartridge - the boot ROM shows its "INSERT GAME" screen - and opens the
ROM browser so you can pick one. Cartridges can be changed at any time from
the File menu or by dragging a file onto the window.

The context of ROMFILE is the filename of the an Atari Lynx ROM dump (can be
either compressed or uncompressed). You can also use switches. By just typing
./handy_sdl you get an overview of the options and the keyboard controls.
WIN32 users should look into the stdout.txt for that information.

This emulator also emulates the Atari Lynx gamebuttons. By using the following
keyboard settings you can (possibly) play a given game:

Lynx joypad          : UP / DOWN / LEFT / RIGHT
Lynx button A        : Z
Lynx button B        : X
Lynx button PAUSE    : RETURN
Lynx button OPTION 1 : F1
Lynx button OPTION 2 : F2

Note that A is the left hand key and B the right hand one, which is the
opposite way round to the letters on the console itself.

With ESC you can easily stop Handy/SDL which will get you back to the prompt
of your shell. There is no confirmation, and the emulator has no other
hotkeys: everything else is set on the command line at startup.

These are the defaults. They can be changed while the emulator is running
from the Input menu, which also swaps A and B if you prefer the labels to
match the console.

Game controllers are supported through SDL's controller database, so an
ordinary pad works without any configuration and can be plugged in or
unplugged while running. The left stick acts as a d-pad in addition to
whatever the d-pad itself is bound to.


------------
- Settings -
------------

Settings are kept in ~/.config/handy-sdl/config (or under XDG_CONFIG_HOME if
that is set) and written when the emulator exits normally. It is plain
key=value text and safe to edit by hand.

Stored are the window size, fullscreen, scaling options, throttle, sound and
FPS counter, the ROM browser directory and recent list, the ComLynx settings,
and the input bindings.

Command line options always override the stored values. The ComLynx link only
comes back automatically if it was running when the emulator last exited;
otherwise the settings are simply restored into the panel without opening a
socket.

-------------------
- A note on sound -
-------------------

By default, the Lynx sound is turned on, but the quality is a bit bad
in combination with the throttle code. It's not that bad, so at least try
the sound support and test it on your system.

